```
     1→---
     2→name: codex
     3→description: Use when the user asks to run Codex CLI (codex exec, codex resume) or wants Codex to review PRs, plans, or specs. Delegates to GPT-5.3-codex with preset reasoning modes.
     4→---
     5→
     6→# Codex Skill
     7→
     8→Run OpenAI Codex CLI for code analysis, reviews, and automated editing. Defaults to `gpt-5.3-codex` with preset reasoning effort per mode.
     9→
    10→## Quick Start
    11→
    12→```bash
    13→# PR review (posts inline comments to GitHub)
    14→/codex review #123
    15→
    16→# Plan review
    17→/codex review-plan @plan-slug
    18→/codex review-plan ./path/to/plan.md
    19→
    20→# Spec review
    21→/codex review-spec @spec-slug
    22→
    23→# General task
    24→/codex "refactor the parser module for clarity"
    25→
    26→# Resume last session
    27→/codex resume
    28→/codex resume "follow-up prompt"
    29→```
    30→
    31→## Modes and Reasoning Presets
    32→
    33→| Mode | Reasoning | Sandbox | Output |
    34→|------|-----------|---------|--------|
    35→| `review` (PR) | `high` | `danger-full-access` | Inline GitHub comments via `gh` + `/tmp` |
    36→| `review-plan` | `high` | `danger-full-access` | Feedback file in `/tmp` |
    37→| `review-spec` | `high` | `danger-full-access` | Feedback file in `/tmp` |
    38→| general (default) | `medium` | `danger-full-access` | Feedback file in `/tmp` |
    39→| general with edits | `medium` | `danger-full-access` | Direct file changes |
    40→
    41→**Model:** `gpt-5.3-codex` unless the user specifies otherwise.
    42→
    43→**Sandbox:** `danger-full-access` for all modes. Codex has full filesystem and network access — same trust level as Claude. This lets it use `gh` for GitHub API calls and `kspec` for spec/task operations directly.
    44→
    45→Users can override reasoning (`-c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"`) or model (`-m gpt-5.2`) explicitly.
    46→
    47→## PR Review Mode
    48→
    49→```
    50→/codex review #123
    51→/codex review @task-ref
    52→```
    53→
    54→### Resolving @task-ref to PR Number
    55→
    56→When the user provides a `@task-ref` instead of a PR number:
    57→
    58→```bash
    59→# Check task for vcs_refs
    60→kspec task get @task-ref --json | jq '.vcs_refs'
    61→
    62→# Or search for PR by task trailer in body/commits
    63→gh pr list --search "Task: @task-ref" --json number,url,title
    64→```
    65→
    66→If no PR is found, error: "No PR found for task @task-ref. Create a PR first with /pr."
    67→
    68→### Flow
    69→
    70→For straightforward diff review, prefer the native `codex exec review` subcommand:
    71→
    72→```bash
    73→codex exec \
    74→  -m gpt-5.3-codex \
    75→  -c model_reasoning_effort="high" \
    76→  -s danger-full-access \
    77→  --skip-git-repo-check \
    78→  review \
    79→  "$(cat <<'PROMPT'
    80→Also check for:
    81→- Own AC coverage: every spec AC should have test with `// AC: @spec-ref ac-N` annotation
    82→- Trait AC coverage: run `kspec item get @spec-ref` to find inherited trait ACs. Each needs test with `// AC: @trait-slug ac-N` annotation
    83→- Run `kspec validate` — any trait AC coverage warnings are MUST-FIX
    84→- Spec alignment: implementation matches spec intent
    85→- Code quality: duplicated logic, ignored shared utilities, inconsistent patterns
    86→- Test quality: no fluff tests, prefer E2E over unit
    87→
    88→After reviewing, post your review to GitHub using `gh pr review <NUMBER>`.
    89→For inline comments, write a JSON body to /tmp/codex-pr-review-body.json with this structure:
    90→  {"event":"COMMENT","body":"summary","comments":[{"path":"src/file.ts","line":42,"body":"comment"}]}
    91→Then post: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<NUMBER>/reviews --method POST --input /tmp/codex-pr-review-body.json
    92→
    93→Also write the full review to /tmp/codex-pr-<NUMBER>-review.md
    94→
    95→Severity levels: MUST-FIX, SHOULD-FIX, SUGGESTION.
    96→PROMPT
    97→)"
    98→```
    99→
   100→For PR reviews that need additional context (task ref, spec ACs), use `codex exec` instead:
   101→
   102→```bash
   103→codex exec \
   104→  -m gpt-5.3-codex \
   105→  -c model_reasoning_effort="high" \
   106→  -s danger-full-access \
   107→  --skip-git-repo-check \
   108→  "$(cat <<'PROMPT'
   109→Review PR #123 in this repository.
   110→
   111→1. Get the PR diff: `gh pr diff 123`
   112→2. Get PR details: `gh pr view 123 --json title,body,files`
   113→3. If the PR body contains a `Task: @task-ref` trailer, get the linked spec:
   114→   `kspec task get @task-ref` and check acceptance criteria coverage
   115→4. Review the code for:
   116→   - Correctness and potential bugs
   117→   - Own AC coverage: every spec AC should have test with `// AC: @spec-ref ac-N` annotation
   118→   - Trait AC coverage: run `kspec item get @spec-ref` to find inherited trait ACs (under "Inherited from @trait-slug" sections). Each needs test with `// AC: @trait-slug ac-N` annotation
   119→   - Run `kspec validate` — trait AC coverage warnings are MUST-FIX
   120→   - Spec alignment: implementation matches spec intent, not just syntax
   121→   - Code quality: duplicated logic, ignored shared utilities, inconsistent patterns
   122→   - Test quality: no fluff tests, prefer E2E over unit
   123→5. Write a review body JSON file to /tmp/codex-pr-review-body.json:
   124→   {"event":"COMMENT","body":"summary","comments":[{"path":"file","line":N,"body":"comment"}]}
   125→   Then post: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/123/reviews --method POST --input /tmp/codex-pr-review-body.json
   126→6. Also write the full review to /tmp/codex-pr-123-review.md
   127→
   128→If no Task trailer exists, proceed with pure code review (skip AC checks).
   129→
   130→Severity levels: MUST-FIX, SHOULD-FIX, SUGGESTION.
   131→PROMPT
   132→)"
   133→```
   134→
   135→### AC Coverage Check
   136→
   137→When a task ref is provided, Codex is instructed to verify:
   138→- Every own AC has test coverage via `// AC: @spec-ref ac-N` annotations
   139→- Every inherited trait AC has test coverage via `// AC: @trait-slug ac-N` annotations
   140→- `kspec validate` shows no trait AC coverage warnings for the spec
   141→- Missing coverage (own or trait) is flagged as `MUST-FIX`
   142→- Spec alignment: implementation matches spec intent, not just syntax
   143→
   144→### Integration with /pr-review
   145→
   146→For full quality-gate PR review (CI verification, merge decision), use `/pr-review @task-ref` instead. This skill focuses on **code-level review feedback** — use both together:
   147→
   148→```
   149→/codex review #123          # Codex posts inline code review
   150→/pr-review @task-ref        # Full quality gate workflow (AC, CI, merge)
   151→```
   152→
   153→## Plan Review Mode
   154→
   155→```
   156→/codex review-plan @plan-slug
   157→/codex review-plan ./path/to/plan.md
   158→```
   159→
   160→### What Codex Checks
   161→
   162→Codex can read plans directly via `kspec plan get` or from a file path:
   163→
   164→```bash
   165→codex exec \
   166→  -m gpt-5.3-codex \
   167→  -c model_reasoning_effort="high" \
   168→  -s danger-full-access \
   169→  --skip-git-repo-check \
   170→  "Review the plan. Get it with: kspec plan get @plan-slug (or read <path>). $(cat <<'PROMPT'
   171→Evaluate against these criteria and write feedback to /tmp/codex-plan-review.md:
   172→
   173→## Structure
   174→- Has ## Specs section with YAML array?
   175→- Has ## Tasks section with derive_from_specs line?
   176→- Has ## Implementation Notes section?
   177→
   178→## Spec Quality
   179→- Every spec has `title` (required)
   180→- Every spec has `acceptance_criteria` array (strongly recommended)
   181→- Each AC has id, given, when, then fields
   182→- AC `then` clauses are concrete and testable (not vague like "works correctly")
   183→- AC are independently testable (not compound)
   184→- `parent` references use @ prefix and point to valid specs
   185→- `traits` reference valid trait names with @ prefix
   186→- `type` is a valid item type (feature, requirement, constraint, decision)
   187→
   188→## Completeness
   189→- Implementation notes present for complex specs
   190→- No obvious gaps in AC coverage (behaviors described but not covered by AC)
   191→- Specs don't overlap or duplicate each other
   192→- Dependencies between specs are explicit
   193→
   194→## Red Flags
   195→- Vague AC: "then: system behaves correctly"
   196→- Untestable AC: "then: performance is acceptable"
   197→- Over-granular AC: one AC per line of code
   198→- Compound AC: "X and Y and Z" in single criterion
   199→- Missing parent for nested items
   200→- Trait applied to fewer than 3 specs
   201→
   202→Format: Use ## headers for sections, bullet lists for issues.
   203→Rate severity: MUST-FIX, SHOULD-FIX, SUGGESTION.
   204→PROMPT
   205→)"
   206→```
   207→
   208→### Output
   209→
   210→Feedback is written to `/tmp/codex-plan-review.md`. After Codex completes:
   211→
   212→```bash
   213→# Show the user the output path
   214→echo "Plan review written to /tmp/codex-plan-review.md"
   215→
   216→# Optionally display summary
   217→head -50 /tmp/codex-plan-review.md
   218→```
   219→
   220→## Spec Review Mode
   221→
   222→```
   223→/codex review-spec @spec-slug
   224→/codex review-spec @module-slug    # Review entire module
   225→```
   226→
   227→### What Codex Checks
   228→
   229→Codex can read specs directly via `kspec item get` and validate with `kspec validate`:
   230→
   231→```bash
   232→codex exec \
   233→  -m gpt-5.3-codex \
   234→  -c model_reasoning_effort="high" \
   235→  -s danger-full-access \
   236→  --skip-git-repo-check \
   237→  "Review the spec. $(cat <<'PROMPT'
   238→Get the spec with: kspec item get @spec-slug
   239→For module-level review: kspec item list --module @module-slug to get all items, then kspec item get each one.
   240→Also run kspec validate for structural issues.
   241→
   242→Evaluate and write feedback to /tmp/codex-spec-review.md:
   243→
   244→## Acceptance Criteria Quality
   245→- Are all ACs concrete and independently testable?
   246→- Do `then` clauses describe observable outcomes (not internal state)?
   247→- Are ACs atomic (not compound "X and Y and Z")?
   248→- Is coverage complete (all described behaviors have ACs)?
   249→- Are there ACs that overlap or contradict each other?
   250→
   251→## Structure
   252→- Appropriate item type for content?
   253→- Description clearly explains purpose?
   254→- References (depends_on, implements, relates_to) are valid?
   255→- Status fields consistent (maturity vs implementation)?
   256→
   257→## Trait Usage
   258→- Applied traits are appropriate (behavior applies to this spec)?
   259→- Cross-cutting behaviors that appear 3+ times should use traits
   260→- Trait ACs don't conflict with spec-specific ACs
   261→
   262→## Testability
   263→- Can each AC be validated with automated tests?
   264→- Are preconditions (given) realistic and reproducible?
   265→- Are actions (when) specific enough to implement?
   266→
   267→Format: ## headers, bullet lists, severity ratings.
   268→MUST-FIX | SHOULD-FIX | SUGGESTION
   269→PROMPT
   270→)"
   271→```
   272→
   273→### Output
   274→
   275→Written to `/tmp/codex-spec-review.md`.
   276→
   277→## General Mode
   278→
   279→```
   280→/codex "analyze the error handling patterns in src/cli/"
   281→/codex "refactor parser module for clarity"
   282→```
   283→
   284→### Defaults
   285→
   286→- **Model:** `gpt-5.3-codex`
   287→- **Reasoning:** `medium`
   288→- **Sandbox:** `danger-full-access`
   289→
   290→### Command Assembly
   291→
   292→```bash
   293→codex exec \
   294→  -m gpt-5.3-codex \
   295→  -c model_reasoning_effort="medium" \
   296→  -s danger-full-access \
   297→  --skip-git-repo-check \
   298→  "Your prompt here. Write output to /tmp/codex-output.md"
   299→```
   300→
   301→## Resuming Sessions
   302→
   303→```bash
   304→# Resume last session with new prompt (positional arg)
   305→codex exec --skip-git-repo-check resume --last "follow-up prompt"
   306→
   307→# Resume last session with prompt from stdin
   308→echo "follow-up prompt" | codex exec --skip-git-repo-check resume --last -
   309→
   310→# Resume with no additional prompt
   311→codex exec --skip-git-repo-check resume --last
   312→```
   313→
   314→When resuming, config flags (`-c`, `-m`, `-s`) are accepted and override the original session's settings. All flags must go between `exec` and `resume`.
   315→
   316→## Output Convention
   317→
   318→All Codex output is written to `/tmp/codex-*` files:
   319→
   320→| Mode | Output file |
   321→|------|------------|
   322→| PR review | `/tmp/codex-pr-{number}-review.md` |
   323→| Plan review | `/tmp/codex-plan-review.md` |
   324→| Spec review | `/tmp/codex-spec-review.md` |
   325→| General | `/tmp/codex-output.md` |
   326→
   327→After every Codex run:
   328→1. Report the output file path to the user
   329→2. Summarize key findings
   330→3. For PR reviews, also post comments to GitHub via `gh`
   331→
   332→## Error Handling
   333→
   334→- If `codex exec` exits non-zero, report the error and ask the user how to proceed
   335→- If output file is empty or missing, Codex may have hit a context or timeout issue — re-run without stderr suppression to diagnose
   336→- For review modes, do NOT suppress stderr — review errors (auth failures, API issues) should be visible
   337→- For general mode, append `2>/dev/null` to suppress thinking tokens unless debugging
   338→
   339→### Preflight Checks
   340→
   341→Before PR review, verify `gh` auth is working:
   342→
   343→```bash
   344→gh auth status
   345→```
   346→
   347→If auth fails, instruct the user to run `gh auth login` before retrying.
   348→
   349→## Overrides
   350→
   351→Users can override any preset:
   352→
   353→```
   354→/codex review #123 --model gpt-5.2 -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"
   355→/codex "prompt" -c model_reasoning_effort="high"
   356→```
   357→
   358→Parse overrides from the user's message and map to CLI flags:
   359→- `--model X` or `-m X` → `-m X`
   360→- `--effort X` → `-c model_reasoning_effort="X"` (translate to config flag)
   361→
   362→## Handling Complex Prompts
   363→
   364→When prompts contain shell-unsafe characters (quotes, backticks, special characters, or multi-line content), use the **prompt-file pattern** instead of inline quoting:
   365→
   366→```bash
   367→# 1. Write prompt to temp file (use mktemp for safety)
   368→PROMPT_FILE=$(mktemp /tmp/codex-prompt.XXXXXX)
   369→cat > "$PROMPT_FILE" << 'EOF'
   370→Review the code for:
   371→- Edge cases with special chars like `$var` and "quoted" strings
   372→- Multi-line logic that's hard to escape inline
   373→- Patterns matching {curly} braces
   374→EOF
   375→
   376→# 2. Use $(cat file) to inject the prompt
   377→codex exec \
   378→  -m gpt-5.3-codex \
   379→  -c model_reasoning_effort="medium" \
   380→  -s danger-full-access \
   381→  --skip-git-repo-check \
   382→  "$(cat "$PROMPT_FILE")"
   383→
   384→# 3. Clean up
   385→rm -f "$PROMPT_FILE"
   386→```
   387→
   388→### When to Use This Pattern
   389→
   390→| Prompt Content | Approach |
   391→|----------------|----------|
   392→| Simple one-liner | Inline quotes work fine |
   393→| Contains `$`, backticks, quotes | Use prompt file |
   394→| Multi-line with formatting | Use prompt file |
   395→| Generated or dynamic content | Use prompt file |
   396→
   397→### Why Prompt-File Over Heredocs?
   398→
   399→Quoted heredocs (`<<'DELIM'...DELIM`) handle quotes, backticks, and `$` literally — they don't cause parsing failures. However, the prompt-file pattern offers practical advantages:
   400→
   401→- **Delimiter collision**: If your prompt contains the delimiter string itself, heredocs fail
   402→- **Mixed expansion**: When you need `$var` expanded in some places but literal in others, heredocs require awkward escaping
   403→- **Debuggability**: You can `cat "$PROMPT_FILE"` to verify exact content before running
   404→- **Reusability**: Same prompt file can be used across multiple commands
   405→
   406→For simple prompts, heredocs work fine. Use prompt-files when prompts are complex, generated dynamically, or need inspection before use.
   407→
   408→## Tips
   409→
   410→- Always use `--skip-git-repo-check` — this project uses a shadow branch worktree that confuses Codex's git detection
   411→- For large diffs, pipe through `head -2000` before giving to Codex to stay within context
   412→- Codex has full access and can run `gh`, `kspec`, `npm`, etc. directly — instruct it to use these tools in your prompt
   413→- Tell Codex to run `kspec` from the project root, not from inside `.kspec/` (same gotcha agents hit)
   414→- Use `codex exec review` for straightforward diff reviews (has `--base`, `--commit`, `--uncommitted` flags built in)
   415→- Do NOT use `--full-auto` — it forces `workspace-write` sandbox, overriding `danger-full-access`
   416→- Do NOT use `REQUEST_CHANGES` review event — reviews are posted from the repo owner's account, and GitHub prohibits requesting changes on your own PR. Use `COMMENT` for findings or `APPROVE` when clean.
   417→
```